\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Why Your Neural Network Trains Slowly: Scaling, Normalization and Smarter Optimizers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 {{\NeXTGraphic optimizers_header.png \width10080 \height10080 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Training a neural network means walking downhill on a loss surface. How fast you get to the bottom depends on how the data is prepared and how each step is chosen. This post covers seven techniques that make that walk faster and more stable: feature scaling, batch normalization, mini-batch gradient descent, momentum, RMSProp, Adam and learning rate decay.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 1. Feature scaling\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 The problem.
\f1\b0  Features often live on very different scales. In the Palmer penguins dataset, body mass is measured in thousands of grams, while bill length is a few tens of millimetres. In any squared distance, body mass dominates.\

\f0\b The fix.
\f1\b0  Rescale features before fitting, and apply exactly the same transformation to the test data.\

