\f1  \uc0\u946 
\f5 \uc0\u8321 
\f1 \'b7v + (1 \uc0\u8722  \u946 
\f5 \uc0\u8321 
\f1 )\'b7
\f3 \uc0\u8711 
\f1 L\uc0\u8232 s 
\f2 \uc0\u8592 
\f1  \uc0\u946 
\f5 \uc0\u8322 
\f1 \'b7s + (1 \uc0\u8722  \u946 
\f5 \uc0\u8322 
\f1 )\'b7(
\f3 \uc0\u8711 
\f1 L)\'b2\
Because both start at zero, they are biased early on, so Adam applies bias correction:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \uc0\u118 \u770  = v / (1 \u8722  \u946 
\f5 \uc0\u8321 \u7511 
\f1 )\uc0\u8232 \u349  = s / (1 \u8722  \u946 
\f5 \uc0\u8322 \u7511 
\f1 )\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \uc0\u952  
\f2 \uc0\u8592 
\f1  \uc0\u952  \u8722  \u951  \'b7 \u118 \u770  / (\u8730 \u349  + \u949 )\
The authors suggest \uc0\u946 
\f5 \uc0\u8321 
\f1  = 0.9, \uc0\u946 
\f5 \uc0\u8322 
\f1  = 0.999 and \uc0\u949  = 10
\f5 \uc0\u8315 \u8312 
\f1 . A good mental picture is a heavy ball with friction, which tends to settle into flat minima.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Pros:
\f1\b0  works well with little tuning and handles noisy gradients and differently scaled parameters.\uc0\u8232 
\f0\b Cons:
\f1\b0  two hyperparameters, tends to overshoot more than RMSProp, and the learning rate still matters.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 7. Learning rate decay\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 A large learning rate gets you moving quickly, but near a minimum it makes you bounce around. Learning rate decay starts high and shrinks the step size over time so the optimizer can settle.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Inverse time decay:
\f1\b0  \uc0\u951  = \u951 
\f5 \uc0\u8320 
\f1  / (1 + epoch \'b7 decay)\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Exponential decay:
\f1\b0  \uc0\u951  = \u951 
\f5 \uc0\u8320 
\f1  \'b7 0.95^epoch\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Example.
\f1\b0  With \uc0\u951 
\f5 \uc0\u8320 
\f1  = 0.1 and exponential decay, the rate is about 0.06 after 10 epochs and about 0.035 after 20.\

\f0\b Pros:
\f1\b0  fast early progress and more precise convergence at the end.\uc0\u8232 
\f0\b Cons:
\f1\b0  the schedule adds hyperparameters, and decaying too fast can stall training before you reach a good minimum.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Putting it together\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 A typical recipe is to standardize your inputs, use mini-batch training with Adam, add batch normalization to deeper networks, and decay the learning rate as training progresses. Each technique attacks a different cause of slow training, which is why they combine so well.\
